[Music]  this dodecahedral cage is made of silica  and it's tiny really tiny it was created  by researchers in the States who hoped  that one day it could help in the fight  against cancer  [Music]  to give you a sense of just how small  this nano cage is let's start with a  single grain of salt it's around 300  microns wide compare that to say a red  blood cell that's it there now compare  the red blood cell to something much  smaller like this the HIV virus the  silicon nano cage is a bit more than ten  times smaller than that to make it  researchers use a relatively simple  technique first they need a template for  that they use soap molecules when in  solution the soap molecules form tiny  balls called micelles the size of the  micelles is precisely controlled by the  researchers then they add a silica  precursor to the mix gradually the  negatively charged silica precursors are  attracted to the positively charged  micelle template over time they build up  forming the structure the template could  then be washed or burned out which would  leave a perfect dodecahedral cage what's  more this process happens without any  intervention it's self assembling this  is how the researchers think that the  cages form but until recently they  didn't even know they existed they're so  small it's nearly impossible to snap a  picture of them  to get that glimpse of the nanocages  researchers used cryo-electron  microscopy they put their sample on a  tiny disc and plunge it into liquid  nitrogen flash freezing it then they can  use an electron microscope to take a  peak making images that look a bit like  this they repeated the process over and  over to gather nearly 20,000 images of  the fuzzy looking cages all lying in  slightly different positions at  different angles and orientations now  here's the clever bit a computer can  analyze and average out all the  different images of all the different  orientations of the structure so feed  them into a computer and an algorithm  can reconstruct a 3d representation and  what popped out was a perfect  dodecahedron only 11 nanometers across  studies have shown that ultra-small  silica particles can easily and safely  pass through the body being excreted in  the urine rather than getting stuck in  the liver which can cause side effects  perhaps nanocages like these could be  used as containers for things like  anti-cancer drugs or as ultra small  vehicles to carry diagnostic molecules  but that is still a long way off  you

